This report outlines the complete architecture, hardware topology, and deployment strategy for building an autonomous AI synthesis engine. The system is designed to ingest open-source repositories, extract their structural DNA, and autonomously synthesize novel applications within an isolated container network.1. Infrastructure & Deployment TopologyTo ensure the multi-agent system does not compete for resources, the architecture distributes the workload across a physical home server cluster rather than a single machine.Hardware Cluster AllocationUtilizing a network switch to connect multiple single-board computers creates a resilient, modular environment for the agents to operate independently.NodeHardware ProfileDedicated FunctionNode 1 (Ingestion)Raspberry Pi 4 Model BHosts the Scavenger Agent. Handles Git cloning, file pruning, and API calls to process repository architectures.Node 2 (Brain)Raspberry Pi Compute Module 4Hosts the Innovator Agent and the central file system (Obsidian Vault). Handles all LLM synthesis and conflict resolution.Node 3 (Execution)High-Capacity Node (SSD attached)Hosts the Builder Agent and the Docker Engine. Compiles the synthesized code and spins up the live container network.Core Software StackAgent Logic: Python (utilizing GitPython, pathspec, and LLM SDKs).Inter-Agent Communication: Flask endpoints to trigger handoffs between the Scavenger, Innovator, and Builder.System UI: A React and Tailwind CSS frontend featuring SVG animations to visualize real-time agent activity and container health.CLI Interface: Claude Code, utilized within the development environment to inject constraints and direct the Innovator Agent manually when required.Containerization: Docker and Docker Compose for isolated environment generation.2. The Knowledge Base: BROski Obsidian BrainThe system requires a persistent, strictly formatted memory layer to function. Instead of a traditional relational database, the architecture uses a markdown-based Obsidian vault. This provides the agents with readable structural maps while allowing you to visually explore the generated connections.Directory Structure:/BROski-Obsidian-Brain/Architectures/: The Scavenger drops raw extracted blueprints here./BROski-Obsidian-Brain/Synthesized/: The Innovator drops the combined, conflict-resolved blueprints here, ready for the Builder./BROski-Obsidian-Brain/Logs/: The Builder outputs runtime logs and crash reports during the QA loop.3. Agent Pipeline Implementation PlanPhase 1: Deploy the ScavengerInitialize the Environment: Set up a Python virtual environment on Node 1.Build the Pruning Logic: Implement the os.walk script to clone repositories and strip out .git, node_modules, and asset folders.Integrate the Extractor: Connect the LLM API to read the remaining structural files (e.g., package.json, docker-compose.yml, requirements.txt).Format the Output: Ensure the LLM outputs strict Markdown files and saves them directly to the /Architectures/ directory in the shared vault.Phase 2: Deploy the InnovatorBuild the Retrieval Engine: Write the Python script on Node 2 that monitors the /Architectures/ directory for two or more compatible blueprints.Design the Synthesis Prompt: Architect the strict LLM prompt that forces the model to merge disparate patterns—such as mapping a gamified token system's backend to a separate repository's interactive radial SVG dashboard.Establish API Contracts: Ensure the Innovator defines exactly how the new frontend and backend will communicate in the merged system.Export for Execution: Save the finalized blueprint to the /Synthesized/ directory and trigger a Flask webhook to wake the Builder Agent.Phase 3: Deploy the Builder & QA LoopEnvironment Generation: The Builder Agent on Node 3 reads the /Synthesized/ blueprint and generates the raw .py, .js, and .yml files into a temporary staging directory.Container Spin-up: The agent executes docker-compose up -d autonomously.Automated QA: The Builder monitors the Docker logs for 60 seconds. If a container crashes (e.g., a port conflict or missing dependency), the agent pipes the stack trace back to an LLM, patches the file, and rebuilds the container.Dashboard Integration: Once stable, the Builder updates the React monitoring UI, marking the new novel application as "Live" and providing the local IP and port for access.4. Immediate Next StepsTo move from concept to execution, begin with these three tasks:Initialize the Vault: Create the empty BROski-Obsidian-Brain repository with the required directory structure.Write the Scavenger Script: Start with the Python clone-and-prune script provided previously to test parsing a single, small React/Flask repository.Configure the Cluster: Flash the OS onto your Compute Module and Pi 4s, connect them to your network switch, and ensure they can share the Obsidian vault via NFS (Network File System).